Original commit body from 44d758ba8f0c1cadbac86d831db840829b0b9a99:

rust.py recorded only calls (plus tokio::spawn(task)), so a function used
as a value had no incoming edge at all: a struct field (Cmd { run: h }),
a binding (let f = h; static F: fn() = h;), an array or tuple element (a
dispatch table), an assignment, a call argument (.map(parse),
.unwrap_or_else(default_dir)), a block's tail and a return operand. Each
is now a `references` edge, evidence function_reference.

Resolution follows Rust's scoping, and each rule is there because the
corpus A/B or a test caught the looser version binding the wrong thing:
- A bare name is a function of this file (its own module's, by qualified
  name), else one a `use` imports -- never a same-named function elsewhere
  that nothing imports. A name bound by a let, parameter, closure
  parameter or pattern in an enclosing function is the local.
- A module path (crate::b::handler, and an imported name's use path)
  resolves the module to its file with the existing _InTreeCrates map and
  takes the function of that name in that file. A free function's name
  carries only its inline mod path, so the full text names nothing and the
  name lookup picked one of two same-named handlers by order. Without a
  crate layout only an unambiguous name binds; a std / core / registry
  path names nothing in the repo (`use std::cmp::max` is not a repo max).
- A Type::f path counts only when the repo declares Type: zellij's
  .map(String::from) on a &str had bound a repo impl From<Name> for
  String. Several same-named declarations are narrowed to the caller's
  crate (new _InTreeCrates.crate_dir): ripgrep's crates/regex
  .map_err(Error::regex) had bound crates/pcre2's Error::regex. Still
  several: no edge.
- The candidate set is the analyzer's multi-value kind index, not
  global_symbols, which is keyed by qualified name and keeps one of two
  same-named free functions.

